SU='Sundarā/Sundarī (Śāraṅgadhara daughter in74)';SF='Śāraṅgadhara (Bengal kṣatriya in74)';CH='Citra (Sundarī husband in74)';S4='gup6-story-sundari-rescue'
e,v=ev(74,'1–8','Vyāsa asks Brahmā what Śūrasena did after hearing Indra. Brahmā says the king sends his messengers to bring a Sankaṣṭa practitioner. Searching house to house they find a fine vimāna and a severely diseased caṇḍālī with draining mouth, sores, insects, stench, wasted body and unclean appearance, whom Gaṇeśa’s messengers have come to take. Astonished, royal messengers bow to her and ask the divine servants who she was formerly, how she became like this and what merit accounts for her ascent.',[('Vyāsa',S),('Brahmā',D),('Śūrasena',F),('Śūrasena royal messengers','FAMILY'),(SU,F),('Gaṇeśa',D),('Sundarī rescuing Gaṇeśa messengers','FAMILY'),('Sundarī rescue vimāna','VAHANA')],story=S4,outcome='The royal search encounters a rescue already ordered by Gaṇeśa and elicits a past-life account.')
e,v=ev(74,'9–14','Divine messengers identify her as daughter of the Bengal kṣatriya Śāraṅgadhara, called Sundarā/Sundarī in the verse. They praise her voice, face and beauty through comparisons with Rati and renowned nāyikās and describe men’s sexual agitation on seeing her. She pursues adulterous encounters, costly clothing, ornaments and sensual pleasures, openly known in Bengal like a prostitute, repeatedly deceiving her husband Citra and spending her father’s amassed wealth.',[(SU,F),(SF,F),(CH,F),('Bengal region/city in74',L),('Rati in74 beauty comparison','EPITHET'),('Renowned nāyikās in74 comparison',C),('Sundarī rescuing Gaṇeśa messengers','FAMILY')],story=S4,outcome='Her father and husband are distinct named men; beauty comparisons do not establish identity with Rati or another woman.')
rel(SU,'daughter_of',SF,e,'BIOLOGICAL_SOURCE_DAUGHTER');rel(SU,'spouse_of',CH,e,'MARITAL')
e,v=ev(74,'15–23','One midnight she leaves her sleeping husband, dressed attractively; Citra catches her hand in anger and rebukes her habitual adultery.17 says her anger becomes quiet, then describes intoxication and strength from forbidden food. In darkness she takes a dagger in her right hand, cuts Citra’s belly and goes to the intended lover. An awake neighbour learns what happened and informs the king. Royal messengers wait, seize her on return, bring her to the king and execute her outside at his command. Yama’s messengers take her to terrible hell, where she suffers upside down, bitten by worms and recalling former deeds. At a kalpa’s end she is born an unfortunate caṇḍālī in the mortal world. The source prints5 for the verse in position15.',[(SU,F),(CH,F),('Sundarī intended lover',F),('Witnessing neighbour in74',F),('Bengal executing king in74',F),('Bengal royal execution messengers','FAMILY'),('Yama messengers in74','FAMILY'),('Sundarī killing dagger',W)],story=S4,outcome='Husband-killing, report, royal execution, hell suffering and kalpa-end rebirth are narrated in order.')
rel(SU,'kills',CH,e)
e,v=ev(74,'24–26','In that rebirth she drinks liquor and sleeps during the day. Waking hungry in night’s first watch, she begs at a vow-practitioner’s home, receives food and eats at moonrise. By chance and of her own will she utters Gaṇeśa’s name; immediately Gaṇanātha sends the fine vimāna. The verse in position25 is printed5. Her conduct is not a deliberately undertaken proper vow.',[(SU,F),('Sundarī food-giving vow practitioner',F),('Gaṇeśa',D),('Sundarī rescue vimāna','VAHANA')],story=S4,outcome='Daytime drunken sleep, moonrise food and voluntary name utterance precede actual divine rescue, without a fabricated conscious complete observance.')
power(SU,e,v,grant='Gaṇeśa',mechanism='Gaṇeśa actually sends the rescue vimāna after the narrated moonrise meal and name utterance.',austerity_or_circumstances='Drinks and sleeps by day; begs and eats the practitioner’s food at moonrise; voluntarily utters Gaṇeśa by chance.',notes='An unintended vow-like sequence; not a knowingly completed proper vow or a formal personal boon request.')
e,v=ev(74,'27–37','Royal messengers recount their mission: Indra rode to see Gṛtsamāda, came to Bhṛśuṇḍi with gods, bowed and worshipped, received permission and worship, and headed home. In Śūrasena’s city his vehicle fell through the gaze of a leprous Vaiśya son. Indra told the king that Sankaṣṭa merit would restart it, prompting the search. They propose taking this woman to the king so she can transfer vow merit, afterward gain twice the merit and resume her ascent while Indra’s vehicle returns home. Gaṇeśa’s servants refuse, saying they have no order from him to give her to someone else.',[('Śūrasena royal messengers','FAMILY'),('Indra',D),('Gṛtsamada',S),('Bhṛśuṇḍī',S),('Gods',C),('Śūrasena',F),('Leprous Vaiśya son in stalled-vimāna frame',F),('Indra stalled vimāna','VAHANA'),(SU,F),('Sundarī rescuing Gaṇeśa messengers','FAMILY')],story=S4,outcome='Merit transfer and doubling are only a royal-emissary proposal; divine messengers refuse without Gaṇeśa’s authorization.')
val('Śūrasena royal messengers','proposed_merit_transfer_74','They propose Sundarī transfer vow merit to restart Indra’s vehicle and receive doubled merit afterward74.33–36; the divine messengers refuse37, so no actual gift is asserted.',e)
e,v=ev(74,'38–45','The servants place her on the vimāna; she immediately gains divine radiance, clothing and ornaments and is taken toward Gaṇeśa amid divine music. The royal messengers return to Śūrasena and report. Her shining vehicle passes, her gaze falls on Indra’s craft, and contact with her craft’s air raises it while people and divine sages watch astonished. Indra actually goes to Amarāvatī and all return to their places; she, with divine body and freed from sin through Sankaṣṭa merit, reaches Vaināyaka’s abode. Properly hearing or carefully telling this story gives all desired objects and removes distress.',[(SU,F),('Sundarī rescuing Gaṇeśa messengers','FAMILY'),('Sundarī rescue vimāna','VAHANA'),('Śūrasena royal messengers','FAMILY'),('Śūrasena',F),('Indra',D),('Indra stalled vimāna','VAHANA'),('Amarāvatī',L),('Divine sages','FAMILY'),('Caṇḍālī-account hearing/telling beneficiaries','FAMILY')],story=S4,outcome='Actual divine-body rescue and abode, plus gaze/vehicle-air contact restarting Indra’s craft, occur without the rejected merit-transfer transaction.')
power(SU,e,v,grant='Gaṇeśa',mechanism='Actual divine body and attainment of Vaināyaka’s abode, free from sin through Sankaṣṭa merit.',notes='The rescue has an actual ending; the text does not state she performed all prescribed vow services consciously.')
power('Indra',e,v,mechanism='His stalled vehicle rises after her gaze falls on it and her vehicle’s air touches it; he actually reaches Amarāvatī.',notes='No accepted voluntary transfer or doubled merit is narrated. Both gaze and contact with the passing vehicle’s air are preserved.')
power('Caṇḍālī-account hearing/telling beneficiaries',e,v,mechanism='All desired objects and removal of distress.',trigger='Proper hearing or careful telling of this account.')
S5='gup6-story-surasena-city-rescue'
e,v=ev(75,'1–8','Having heard and seen the vow’s greatness, Śūrasena asks Vasiṣṭha for a suitable time to practice a vow giving immediate assurance. The sage prescribes Māgha dark fourth on Tuesday. The devoted king and unnamed wife assemble supplies. On that day he bathes, finishes daily rites, worships Gaṇeśa and honours Brahmins and Vasiṣṭha with permission. He concentrates on Gaṇeśa and japs the name, standing on one big toe until sunset. He bathes again, observes evening worship and starts proper pūjā with Vasiṣṭha.',[('Brahmā',D),('Śūrasena',F),('Vasiṣṭha',S),('Śūrasena unnamed wife in75',F),('Gaṇeśa',D),('Śūrasena vow Brahmins','FAMILY')],story=S5,outcome='Actual timed observance and guru-guided services follow the earlier exemplary rescue.')
rel('Śūrasena unnamed wife in75','spouse_of','Śūrasena',e,'MARITAL');rel('Vasiṣṭha','instructs','Śūrasena',e);rel('Śūrasena','worships','Gaṇeśa',e)
e,v=ev(75,'9–21','The king prepares a great banana-pillared pavilion with clothing, ornaments, parasols, fly-whisks, mirrors, flowers, gems and lamps. A beautiful, well-proportioned gold Gaṇeśa image stands on a gold kalaśa. Amid Brahmin recitation, song, dance and instruments he performs Vedic and Purāṇic sixteen-service worship with pañcāmṛta, modaka/apūpa/laḍḍū/pāyasa and sugar, varied dishes, scented water, fruits, areca/betel, jewels and gold dakṣiṇā, dūrvā, light and mantra-flowers. He gives arghya to tithi, Gaṇeśa and Moon, honours and feeds Brahmins and gives ten thousand cows, clothes, ornaments and fees. He eats with friends and kin and keeps night vigil with song, instruments and stories. At dawn he bathes and worships again, then gives the image, furnishings and dakṣiṇā to Vasiṣṭha. Pleased, Gaṇeśa sends a prosperous vimāna.',[('Śūrasena',F),('Vasiṣṭha',S),('Gaṇeśa',D),('Śūrasena vow Brahmins','FAMILY'),('Śūrasena gold ritual image in75',C),('Śūrasena royal rescue vimāna','VAHANA'),('Moon',D),('Sankaṣṭa caturthī tithi',D),('Dūrvā',C),('Śūrasena friends and kin in75','FAMILY')],story=S5,outcome='Complete actual ritual and donations precede the deity’s sent vehicle; the exact ten-thousand-cow gift is retained.')
power('Śūrasena',e,v,grant='Gaṇeśa',mechanism='The pleased deity sends a prosperous rescue vimāna after the king’s actual observance and final image-gift.',trigger='The described proper vow and completion worship/gift.',notes='Not yet an arrival at the final abode; collective boarding and the new obstruction follow.')
e,v=ev(75,'22–33','The messengers place the king, bearing Gaṇeśa’s form, aboard while people rejoice. They say pleased Vināyaka sent them, eager to see him. The king weeps with joy and says the unfathomable, unmanifest Lord beyond speech and mind, praised by Vedas, śāstras and deities, has no need to see him; his being remembered is great fortune. The messengers admit devotees make the formless deity assume form. The king expresses a great wish: how can he leave his city for Gaṇeśa when he has not even consumed Hālāhala apart from its people? He cannot enjoy highest happiness without them. They agree his wish should be fulfilled, fearing Gaṇeśa’s anger if they refuse.',[('Śūrasena',F),('Śūrasena royal divine messengers','FAMILY'),('Śūrasena royal rescue vimāna','VAHANA'),('Gaṇeśa',D),('Śūrasena city beings','FAMILY')],story=S5,outcome='The king requests collective inclusion; messengers promise accommodation before the later actual lift attempt.')
val('Śūrasena','collective_rescue_request','Take the city’s beings with him; he cannot enjoy supreme happiness without them75.31–32. Hālāhala wording is his stated solidarity comparison, not a separately narrated poisoning episode.',e)
e,v=ev(75,'34–45','The messengers immediately put all the city’s beings aboard, explicitly all caturākaraja creatures, with divine clothes and ornaments. Some wonder how they gained this without their own merit; others attribute rescue to the king’s merit, comparing metal becoming gold through a treasure and sinners attaining perfection through saintly speech. The attendants attempt upward movement, but the craft remains inert on earth. Some spot a leprous man and urge discarding him, saying the craft will rise when he descends. Śūrasena instead offers to exclude himself and carry all others, or asks the man’s former birth, sin and a compassionate remedy. The messengers reassure him and undertake to explain. Numbering prints15 at position35 and4 at41.',[('Śūrasena',F),('Śūrasena royal divine messengers','FAMILY'),('Śūrasena city beings','FAMILY'),('Śūrasena royal rescue vimāna','VAHANA'),('Leprous Vaiśya son in stalled-vimāna frame',F)],story=S5,outcome='All classes are accommodated, but lift actually fails. The king refuses the proposed abandonment and explicitly seeks remedy or his own exclusion.')
val('Śūrasena city beings','boarded_scope','All caturākaraja creatures, not only humans75.34.',e)
S6='gup6-story-budha-pastlife';BU='Budha (Dūrva son in76)';DF='Dūrva (Budha father in76)';MO='Śākinī (Budha mother in76)';WI='Sāvitrī (Budha wife in76)';PRO='Unnamed prostitute in Budha account76';BH='Bhīma (elderly informant in76)';KA='Kalabha (Sulabhā husband in76)';SL='Sulabhā (Kalabha wife in76)'
e,v=ev(76,'1–8','The messengers describe a learned ascetic Brahmin devoted to gods and Brahmins in Gauḍa city. His son’s mother is Śākinī; the son’s faithful wife is Sāvitrī. An only-child affection leads to adornments, great beauty compared with Rati’s husband, and parents unwilling to endure separation. In youth he abandons his wife for adultery, slander and disregard for paternal counsel. When an unnamed captivating prostitute comes, he puts away his ornaments before his parents, then steals them and gives them to her. He lives absorbed in her amid scents and pleasures, compared in dedication with a Brahman-focused yogin.',[(BU,F),(DF,S),(MO,F),(WI,F),(PRO,F),('Gauḍa region/city in76',L),('Rati husband in76 comparison','EPITHET'),('Śūrasena royal divine messengers','FAMILY')],story=S6,outcome='The source introduces distinct mother and wife before the son’s abandonment and theft; the unnamed prostitute is not merged with Sundarī74.')
rel(BU,'son_of',DF,e,'BIOLOGICAL_SOURCE_SON');rel(BU,'son_of',MO,e,'BIOLOGICAL_SOURCE_SON');rel(WI,'spouse_of',BU,e,'MARITAL');rel(MO,'spouse_of',DF,e,'MARITAL')
e,v=ev(76,'9–18','The distressed father searches every house for his son without eating or drinking, but cannot see him. At midnight he tells his wife their home is empty without Budha and asks when life-preserving sight will return. Śākinī says hunger, thirst, anxiety and grief exhaust her and only seeing the boy will sustain her. Dūrva takes his staff and searches again. An elderly man of lowest caste named Bhīma tells him Budha is playing in the prostitute’s home, passion-bound and disregarding parentage, and says his grief is wasted. Dūrva asks how his son could become absorbed there and goes to that house.',[(DF,S),(MO,F),(BU,F),(BH,F),(PRO,F)],story=S6,outcome='Source explicitly names the father Dūrva and son Budha and gives Bhīma’s information; no Pāṇḍava or planetary identity is inferred.')
e,v=ev(76,'19–31','The father seizes the intoxicated, red-eyed son’s hand and rebukes him, preferring a thorn-tree or stone to such a son and questioning his life. Budha strikes the father’s face, insults him for obstructing his pleasure and kicks him to death.24 reports Hareśvara with so’vadat at the father’s death; its speaker is not assigned without support. The son rejoices, binds the father’s foot, throws the body away, drinks and returns to the prostitute. Next morning Śākinī joyfully embraces him, with milk flowing through affection, asks where he was, says both parents waited without food/water, and repeatedly urges him to find his searching father. Angered, he hits her head with dry wood, knows her unconscious, ties and throws her outside and returns to the prostitute. Good people cremate both parents with their wood. The king does not punish him because he is a Brahmin.21 is printed2.',[(DF,S),(MO,F),(BU,F),(PRO,F),('People cremating Budha parents','FAMILY'),('Gauḍa king in76',F),('Hareśvara utterance in76','EPITHET')],story=S6,outcome='Both parents are killed and cremated, and royal nonpunishment is explicitly explained. The single death-utterance speaker remains unresolved.')
rel(BU,'kills',DF,e);rel(BU,'kills',MO,e)
e,v=ev(76,'32–42','When Budha returns, wife Sāvitrī gently urges virtuous conduct suited to his respected birth: act now for future-world happiness, in youth for old age, during eight months for the rains and by day for the night. She says the king excuses him through affection for his father and his Brahmin status, asks why he abandoned his faithful, beautiful and compliant lawful wife for a prostitute, and describes public condemnation, fear of speaking to him and her shame. She proposes marital companionship rather than public wrongdoing, urges him to leave the prostitute and abandon ignorant or negligent deeds, and says continuing otherwise loses both this world and the next. Her words pierce Budha deeply.',[(WI,F),(BU,F),(DF,S),('Gauḍa king in76',F),(PRO,F)],story=S6,outcome='A detailed wife-to-husband corrective conversation precedes his threat and her death; no new woman or mother identity is substituted.')
e,v=ev(76,'43–49','Enraged, Budha insults Sāvitrī and threatens her with his parents’ fate. She says one who did not respect his educating parents cannot protect her, and regards death at her husband’s hand as better for a virtuous wife. He seizes her hair and beats her with wood, clods, fists and stones. Through prior merit she remembers Rāma in husband-form and dies from his blows to vital parts. She goes to heaven with divine body and enjoys highest happiness. He drags her by the foot and throws her far away at night, then tells the prostitute he destroyed everyone for her and resumes pleasure.',[(BU,F),(WI,F),(PRO,F),('Rāma remembered in husband form in76','EPITHET')],story=S6,outcome='Wife-killing and her actual heavenly/divine-body destination are narrated; her remembrance does not establish identity with the Rāma born in the next chapter.')
rel(BU,'kills',WI,e)
power(WI,e,v,mechanism='After being killed she actually goes to heaven in divine body and enjoys highest happiness.',notes='Her Rāma remembrance is attributed to prior merit; chronology alone is not promoted to a new personal boon or an explicit sole causal trigger.')
e,v=ev(76,'50–62','After much time Budha visits sage Kalabha’s house while the sage bathes, seizes his beautiful wife by the hair, embraces, kisses and rapes her. Sulabhā says her parents’ given name has made her easy to him, condemns the assault in her husband’s absence and curses him to become a leper in another birth whose very name people will avoid. He flees frightened to the prostitute but resumes drink and pleasure. Brahmā declines further wrongdoings, saying telling others’ faults diminishes merit. Budha eventually dies; Yama orders messengers to throw him into hell until the elemental dissolution. Having endured that, he is born in a Vaiśya home, severely leprous through the sage’s wife’s curse. The discourse condemns his deeds, prescribes clothed bathing after touching him and avoidance of his name, and says removing him will raise the craft.',[(BU,F),(KA,S),(SL,F),('Yama',D),('Yama messengers in76','FAMILY'),('Brahmā',D),(PRO,F),('Leprous Vaiśya son in stalled-vimāna frame',F)],story=S6,outcome='The curse specifies a later birth; actual hell and Vaiśya-leper rebirth are narrated, ending the past-life explanation before the king requests mercy.')
rel(SL,'spouse_of',KA,e,'MARITAL');rel(BU,'rapes',SL,e);rel('Leprous Vaiśya son in stalled-vimāna frame','source_rebirth_of',BU,e,'SOURCE_EXPLICIT_REBIRTH')
p_curse_budha=power(BU,e,v,grant=SL,granted='Curse: leprosy in another birth and avoidance of his name.',trigger='His rape of Sulabhā during Kalabha’s absence.',duration='The curse targets another birth; no fixed year-count.',notes='A curse, not favourable personal boon. Actual later Vaiśya-leper birth is narrated59; dissolution qualifies the preceding hell stay58, not silently the curse duration.')
e,v=ev(76,'63–68','Hearing the messengers’ account, Śūrasena trembles, says he did not know these acts, rises and bows anxiously. He asks compassionately for a way to remove all the wrongdoer’s defects. They tell him to rise and listen: Gaṇeśa has a famous FOUR-syllable name; japa it into the man’s ear and all faults will cease like darkness at sunrise. They say he is not qualified for other remedies, so the name alone should be japped.',[('Śūrasena',F),('Śūrasena royal divine messengers','FAMILY'),('Leprous Vaiśya son in stalled-vimāna frame',F),('Gaṇeśa',D),('Four-syllable Gajānana name in76',T)],story=S5,outcome='The king seeks restoration, and the servants prescribe an auditory name remedy with an explicit limitation.')
e,v=ev(76,'69–75','At the messengers’ direction Śūrasena japs the four-syllable name into the Vaiśya’s ear THREE times, with jayapūrvam prefix wording retained. Merely hearing Gajānana, the man gains a divine body shining like the dawn sun. All sin ends through hearing the name alone; he boards the craft. By Vighnarāja’s command, the messengers immediately bring that vehicle with all its beings to Vaināyaka’s abode. Brahmā closes the answers and praises Sankaṣṭa-caturthī as righteous, fame- and life-giving, granting accomplishment by hearing, quelling suffering and obstacles, and the power of dūrvā and the name.',[('Śūrasena',F),('Śūrasena royal divine messengers','FAMILY'),('Leprous Vaiśya son in stalled-vimāna frame',F),('Śūrasena city beings','FAMILY'),('Śūrasena royal rescue vimāna','VAHANA'),('Gaṇeśa',D),('Four-syllable Gajānana name in76',T),('Brahmā',D),('Sankaṣṭa-account hearing beneficiaries','FAMILY')],story=S5,outcome='Actual hearing removes sin, gives divine body and permits collective final transport; the previously proposed exclusion is not enacted.')
