e,v=ev(77,'1–2','Vyāsa asks who else practiced the vow. Brahmā says Jāmadagnya Rāma practiced it and gained fame, victory, knowledge and long life. Vyāsa requests the full origin account: how Rāma was born, of which father and mother.',[('Vyāsa',S),('Brahmā',D),(RA,F)],story=S7,outcome='A brief retrospective vow result introduces a new parentage narrative, not a completed revenge sequence here.')
power(RA,e,v,mechanism='Brahmā reports fame, victory, knowledge and long life through the earlier vow.',trigger='His reported vow practice.',notes='No exact duration or direct boon request/grant scene in this terse prefatory report.')
e,v=ev(77,'3–12','In Śvetadvīpa Jamadagni is a great sage, knowing three times and performing creation/destruction, restraint/favour through mind, frightening gods. His wife is explicitly Reṇukā, whose beauty exceeds Kāma’s wife.6 says she became famed as Rati; this source’s Reṇukā/Rati description is kept distinct from automatically identifying Kāma’s wife. Poetic comparisons describe creatures seeking her eye-beauty and Moon serving Īśvara for her face-beauty. She is praised as beginningless/endless goddess, root nature and ruler. Rāma is born to her from Jamadagni, here praised as an actual Īśvara form; the son is compared with Viṣṇu the yoga lord. He is beautiful, strong, obedient to parents, devoted to gods/Brahmins/gurus/wise people/cows/bodhi, learned in Vedas/Vedāṅgas/śāstras/smṛtis, eloquent as Bṛhaspati, forbearing as earth and deep as ocean.',[(JA,S),(RE,F),(RA,F),('Śvetadvīpa',L),('Kāma',D),('Kāma wife in77 comparison','EPITHET'),('Rati name of Reṇukā in77','EPITHET'),('Moon',D),('Īśvara in77 poetic reference','EPITHET'),('Viṣṇu',D),('Bṛhaspati',D),('Gods',C)],story=S7,outcome='Direct father/mother/spouse relationships and attributed divine/poetic descriptions are distinguished from universal biography.')
rel(RE,'spouse_of',JA,e,'MARITAL');rel(RA,'son_of',JA,e,'BIOLOGICAL_SOURCE_SON');rel(RA,'son_of',RE,e,'BIOLOGICAL_SOURCE_SON');rel('Rati name of Reṇukā in77','source_name_of',RE,e)
power(JA,e,v,mechanism='Mental creation/destruction, restraint/favour and three-time knowledge attributed to the sage.',notes='Specific source description, with no narrated new boon grantor or restrictions.')
e,v=ev(77,'13–23','With his parents’ permission Rāma goes to the forest printed Nimeṣāraṇya, firmly intending study of many disciplines. After his departure mighty King Kārtavīrya appears. The source attributes earth-subjection, steady Lakṣmī as a Viṣṇu portion, five-hundred-arrow volleys and formidable strength to him. His innumerable elephants, chariots, horses and infantry cover earth when he moves; his conch frightens enemies, and five hundred hand-claps shake the cosmic egg. In blue clothes with blue parasol and similarly dressed four-part troops, he wanders forests, rivers, forts and mountains and kills some game. On a Sahyādri summit he sees an excellent hermitage and asks servants whose it is.',[(RA,F),(JA,S),(RE,F),(SON,F),('Lakṣmī',D),('Viṣṇu',D),('Nimeṣāraṇya (printed in77)',L),('Kārtavīrya four-part hunting army','ARMY'),('Kārtavīrya conch in77',C),('Sahyādri',L),('Jamadagni hermitage in77',L),('Kārtavīrya hunting servants','FAMILY')],story=S7,outcome='Rāma leaves before Kārtavīrya’s hunting visit; the printed place name is not silently changed to Naimiṣa.')
power(SON,e,v,mechanism='Source describes five-hundred-arrow volleys, frightening conch sound and five hundred hand-claps that shake the cosmic egg.',notes='77.15/18/19 source capacities; no extra opponent or unreported hunt weapon sequence.')
