e,v=ev(77,'24–33','The king’s servant identifies Jamadagni as a sage like a second Sun, capable of curse and favour, whose sight removes crores of sins, and advises a visit. Kārtavīrya holds back his full army, takes selected companions and goes to the sage sitting on kuśa with fire-like radiance. All bow; troops sit in large halls and groves while the king, with four attendants, sits on the sage’s appointed seat. Jamadagni and pupils honour them with foot-water, arghya, seats and cows. Hunt-worn troops bathe in clean cold lakes and hear pupils reciting Vedas and śāstras and debating.',[(SON,F),(JA,S),('Kārtavīrya hunting servants','FAMILY'),('Kārtavīrya four-part hunting army','ARMY'),('Jamadagni pupils','FAMILY'),('Jamadagni hermitage in77',L)],story=S7,outcome='A respectful actual visit and sage hospitality precede the later dispute; full army restraint is stated.')
e,v=ev(77,'34–48','The king praises his parents, birth, knowledge, tapas, fortune, lineage and fame as fruitful through seeing Jamadagni and calls him Para-Brahman, satisfied by hospitality. Though knowing him, the sage asks identity, name and purpose. The king explicitly says he is Kṛtavīrya’s son called Kārtavīrya, with no purpose beyond beneficial sage sight, and asks leave to return. Jamadagni reciprocally praises the visit and insists he eat before departing. The king says he cannot even drink apart from innumerable troops and doubts all can be fed. The sage assures a four-kind meal for the whole army, invites even the home forces and asks him to rest briefly by the river while food is prepared.',[(SON,F),(KV,F),(JA,S),('Kārtavīrya four-part hunting army','ARMY'),('Hermitage waiting riverbank in77',L)],story=S7,outcome='Explicit self-identification supports the local father-child line; the king’s concern for troops produces a promise to feed all.')
val(SON,'explicit_self_identification_77','I am Kṛtavīrya’s son, known as Kārtavīrya77.39; earlier68/71 anomalies remain source-local rather than silently emended.',e)
e,v=ev(77,'49–62','Astonished, the king goes to the riverbank. Jamadagni calls his wife, tells her what happened, and the couple summons and worships Kāmadhenu. They ask her to preserve their honour and truth by instantly feeding the invited king’s innumerable forces according to taste, otherwise their promise would fail and ill fame follow. At their prayer she creates by her own power a great city with beautiful houses, gem-pillared halls, gardens, flags, music, vessel rows, four-kind food, gates and moat, male/female attendants and abundant prepared meals. Guards prevent unauthorized entry under Jamadagni’s rule. Varied soups, pāyasa, cooked dishes, pañcāmṛta, edible/lickable/suckable/drinkable foods, fruits and seasoned dishes are prepared in innumerable bright vessels. Through Kāmadhenu’s satisfaction, the sage summons pupils to invite Kṛtavīrya’s son and troops to eat.',[(SON,F),(JA,S),(RE,F),(KD,C),('Kāmadhenu-created feast city in77',L),('Kāmadhenu-created feast attendants','FAMILY'),('Jamadagni pupils','FAMILY'),('Kārtavīrya four-part hunting army','ARMY')],story=S7,outcome='The sage-wife prayer is answered by Kāmadhenu’s own actual city/food creation; no new Gaṇeśa boon dialogue is invented.')
rel(KD,'creates','Kāmadhenu-created feast city in77',e);rel(JA,'prays_to',KD,e);rel(RE,'prays_to',KD,e)
