e,v=ev(78,'1–8','Jamadagni urgently sends pupils to invite the riverbank king, with blessed greetings and prepared innumerable six-taste dishes. Kārtavīrya and the army bathe and come to a house and gateway he has neither seen nor heard of in three worlds. Staff-bearing guards initially stop him, seeking the sage’s signal, but pupils overrule them and bring him into the hall. The king inwardly wonders at wealth exceeding even Hari, Hara and Brahmā. The sage comes forward and seats guests at their vessels.',[(JA,S),(SON,F),('Jamadagni pupils','FAMILY'),('Kārtavīrya four-part hunting army','ARMY'),('Kāmadhenu-created feast guards','FAMILY'),('Kāmadhenu-created feast city in77',L),('Viṣṇu',D),('Śiva',D),('Brahmā',D)],story=S7,outcome='The vast feast is actually presented, with an initial authorization barrier removed by pupils.')
e,v=ev(78,'9–21','With the king’s permission guests and troops, including outside diners, are seated in rows; the king is seated after everyone. They eat amid music, question unfamiliar tasty dishes, roots and fruits and marvel at preparation within one watch. Pupils give washing-water and tooth-cleaning sticks and leftovers to elephants, horses and bulls. In another large prepared hall people eat sugarcane, grapes, mango, jackfruit and pomegranate and receive cardamom, clove, camphor, khadira/areca and betel. Jamadagni gives everyone suitable clothes and ornaments and especially costly gifts to the king. He modestly asks what a forest sage could provide a king already fulfilling all wishes and praised as a Viṣṇu form; by following his invitation the king has made his fame and the sage’s successful hospitality known, as a great man obeying a smaller man gains saintly reputation.',[(JA,S),(SON,F),('Jamadagni pupils','FAMILY'),('Kārtavīrya four-part hunting army','ARMY'),('Kāmadhenu-created feast city in77',L),('Viṣṇu',D)],story=S7,outcome='Actual feeding of people and animals and subsequent gifts fulfill the invitation before the king’s new demand.9 is printed1.')
e,v=ev(78,'22–32','Astonished, the king asks whether the previously unseen marvel arose through māyā or tapas. Jamadagni says he has never uttered falsehood, even in jest, and truthfully identifies Kāmadhenu as the agent of everything. Brahmā remarks on adverse fate and approaching ill fortune. Though fully fed, the king decides to take the cow. He argues peaceful, desireless, sense-controlled forest sages living on roots, fruit or air, studying Vedas/śāstras and seeking liberation have no need for such riches; a great object belongs with great work and a forest dweller is not suited to the jewel. He demands a willing gift of Kāmadhenu and says she will still be the sage’s when kept with him, then threatens what powerful kings with armies cannot seize at home or abroad.',[(SON,F),(JA,S),(KD,C),('Brahmā',D),('Kārtavīrya four-part hunting army','ARMY')],story=S7,outcome='The agent explanation is Kāmadhenu, not a new Gaṇeśa grant; the king turns hospitality into a coercive gift demand.')
val(SON,'demand_for_kamadhenu','Give the cow willingly; powerful kings can seize objects with their armies78.26–32. No actual voluntary gift follows.',e)
e,v=ev(78,'33–38','Hearing Kārtavīrya’s words, Jamadagni burns with anger like Fire fed ghee and rebukes him with red eyes. He says he invited what he thought a good, pure, great king but did not know hidden crookedness, using heron and cuckoo/crow comparisons. He condemns his own error in seeking royal friendship, a friendship neither seen, heard nor experienced in the world.',[(JA,S),(SON,F),('Fire',D)],story=S7,outcome='Jamadagni rejects the demand and diagnoses deceit in the guest; the dispute remains active into battle.')
e,v=ev(79,'1–8','Jamadagni says the king seeks Kāmadhenu while courting self-destruction, desires the unattainable and risks the sin of destroying three worlds. The king erupts with anger and says only the sage’s Brahmin status made him endure harsh words. He rises and orders messengers to release the cow from her tether and bring her. They surround her, but her foṭkāra blast kills some; her anger-fire burns others. The force of her breath blows other warriors into the sky, covering the Sun and making the scene obscure.',[(JA,S),(SON,F),(KD,C),('Kārtavīrya cow-seizing messengers','FAMILY'),('Sun',D)],story=S7,outcome='Actual coercive seizure attempt triggers three distinct cow counters: fatal foṭkāra, anger-fire and sky-lifting breath.')
power(KD,e,v,mechanism='Foṭkāra blast kills some seizing messengers, anger-fire burns others, breath blows warriors skyward and covers the Sun.',trigger='Royal servants surround her to seize her from the tether.',notes='The named foṭkāra is retained without inventing a different anatomical weapon or external boon source.')
e,v=ev(79,'9–18','Darkness, earth-shaking and falling trees accompany the assault. Troops flee in ten directions. Someone whips the cow from a distance; she leaps and chases the army like lion against elephants or Garuḍa against serpents. Kārtavīrya tells frightened troops he will blow his conch and make her flee, but the mighty world-filling blast does not frighten her. Servants then strike her with staffs and iron. From every struck body location emerge armed, equipped warriors. Śakas and Barbaras arise from her hair; Paṭaccaras from her foot region, with varied Yavana peoples and other warriors also arising. Cavalry, elephants and powerful charioteers join combat against the king’s troops.',[(SON,F),(KD,C),('Kārtavīrya four-part hunting army','ARMY'),('Kārtavīrya cow-seizing messengers','FAMILY'),('Kārtavīrya conch in77',C),('Śaka warriors from Kāmadhenu hair','ARMY'),('Barbara warriors from Kāmadhenu hair','ARMY'),('Paṭaccara warriors from Kāmadhenu feet','ARMY'),('Yavana peoples in Kāmadhenu army','ARMY'),('Other Kāmadhenu-generated warriors','ARMY'),('Garuḍa',D)],story=S7,outcome='The conch counter fails; body strikes produce the cow’s armies with exact stated hair/foot origins.')
power(KD,e,v,mechanism='Warriors with weapons emerge from struck body regions; Śaka/Barbara troops from hair, Paṭaccaras from feet, plus other named troops and cavalry/elephants/chariots.',trigger='Servants strike her with staffs and iron.',counter='The king’s conch does not frighten her79.14.',notes='Yavana origins are not assigned to an unmentioned excretory or other body site.')
for n in ['Śaka warriors from Kāmadhenu hair','Barbara warriors from Kāmadhenu hair']:rel(n,'arises_from_hair_of',KD,e)
rel('Paṭaccara warriors from Kāmadhenu feet','arises_from_feet_of',KD,e)
e,v=ev(79,'19–28','Cow-generated fighters strike down royal troops; more converge and hundreds die through mutual blows. Weapons smash weapons, then wrestling continues amid dust, noise, obscured Sun and inability to distinguish one’s own side. Horses, elephants, chariot wheels, drums, flutes and war cries fill the battle, frightening spirits/Rākṣasas and warriors’ wives while benefiting scavengers. Knees and heads are broken; swords, shields, bhallas, arrows, bows, warriors and chariots fall beyond count. Remaining royal soldiers flee. Cow troops pursue laughing but DO NOT kill the fugitives, rebuking them for attacking a sage who harmed them in no way. The narrator attributes the king’s bad judgment to former fault.',[(SON,F),(KD,C),('Kārtavīrya four-part hunting army','ARMY'),('Other Kāmadhenu-generated warriors','ARMY'),('Śaka warriors from Kāmadhenu hair','ARMY'),('Barbara warriors from Kāmadhenu hair','ARMY'),('Paṭaccara warriors from Kāmadhenu feet','ARMY'),('Yavana peoples in Kāmadhenu army','ARMY'),('Battle swords in79',W),('Battle shields in79',W),('Battle bhallas in79',W),('Battle arrows in79',W),('Battle bows in79',W),('Battle spirits/Rākṣasas in79',C),('Warriors’ wives in79','FAMILY')],story=S7,outcome='The royal army is routed; pursuit is explicit but killing of fleeing troops is expressly withheld.')
e,v=ev(79,'29–34','With his army broken, Kṛtavīrya’s son rises, takes FIVE HUNDRED ARROWS and bows, places his left knee on earth, draws the bows and shoots an arrow-net at the cow army. The number grammatically accompanies arrows; no exact five-hundred-bow count is asserted. The volley is fruitless. Repeated equal volleys break not even one cow warrior. The king despairs at his lost strength. The cow army says one should not strike a distressed opponent and goes to heaven; Kāmadhenu questions combat with the lesser opponent.30 is printed00.',[(SON,F),(KV,F),(KD,C),('Other Kāmadhenu-generated warriors','ARMY'),('Kārtavīrya five-hundred-arrow volley in79',W),('Battle bows in79',W)],story=S7,outcome='Repeated royal volleys actually fail; the opposing force withdraws while refusing to strike the distressed king.')
power(SON,e,v,mechanism='Shoots an arrow-net with five hundred arrows, repeated with equal volleys.',counter='All volleys fruitless; not even one cow warrior is broken79.31–32.',notes='The record preserves actual failed effect rather than universally granting the earlier announced victory capacity.')
boon=next(p for p in d['boons_and_powers']if p.get('grantor_id')==names['Brahmā-led deity boon group in73'])
boon['counter']='Despite the earlier victory grant, his army is routed and repeated volleys fail against Kāmadhenu’s forces79.27–34; no explicit grant revocation or newly stated restriction is narrated.';boon['evidence_ids'].append(e);boon['field_evidence']['counter']=[e];boon['later_event_ids'].append(v)
e,v=ev(79,'35–43','After Kāmadhenu leaves, Kārtavīrya goes to Jamadagni in anger, claims to know his deceit and says a deceitful man should not count as a Brahmin. The KING HIMSELF takes one arrow and pierces the sage; the arrow lodges in his heart and Jamadagni gives up life. Reṇukā protests needless Brahmin-killing. The red-eyed king tells her to be silent or he will kill her too and strikes her with TWENTY-ONE arrows. She remembers Jamadagni and remains alive to ask why he struck innocent parents and predict certain destruction of his arms. Hearing her, the king departs to his city with a small remaining army, anxious, grieving soldiers, reproaching himself and without enthusiasm. This chapter describes a continuous visit sequence;80.21’s later recollection gives a different home-and-return chronology.',[(SON,F),(JA,S),(RE,F),('Kārtavīrya heart-piercing arrow in79',W),('Twenty-one arrows wounding Reṇukā',W),('Kārtavīrya four-part hunting army','ARMY')],story=S7,outcome='The king directly kills Jamadagni, wounds Reṇukā who still speaks, and then returns home; no imported sons kill the sage.')
rel(SON,'kills',JA,e);rel(SON,'wounds',RE,e)
power(SON,e,v,mechanism='Kills Jamadagni with one heart-arrow and wounds Reṇukā with twenty-one arrows.',notes='King is the explicit agent; his sons are not substituted. Reṇukā remains alive through her later reply and80 instructions.')
power(SON,e,v,grant=RE,mechanism='Reṇukā predicts his arms will certainly be destroyed.',notes='Explicit future warning79.41, not an already enacted arm destruction or a newly worded favourable boon.')
pj=next(p for p in d['boons_and_powers']if p['recipient_id']==names[JA])
pj['counter']='His described mental capacities do not prevent the narrated death from the king’s one heart-arrow79.36–37.';pj['evidence_ids'].append(e);pj['field_evidence']['counter']=[e];pj['later_event_ids'].append(v)
e,v=ev(80,'1–14','After the king leaves, the arrow-covered Reṇukā grieves, asking where her sons are and especially her angry beloved Rāma, and says seeing him will precede her departure to the gods’ abode. At her remembrance Rāma comes, sees her arrows and his father dead from Kārtavīrya’s heart-arrow, faints like a wind-felled tree and weeps. He compares the fatherless hermitage with earth lacking Meru, Amarāvatī without Indra and the world without Gaṅgā, laments the parents’ great tapas and mother’s world-burning capacity, and removes her arrows with her head on his lap. Recalling her milk, food, clothing, fruit and attentive care, he condemns life without his parents. The plural sons are unnamed; no names or counts of brothers are imported.',[(RE,F),(RA,F),(JA,S),(SON,F),('Reṇukā sons mentioned collectively in80','FAMILY'),('Meru',L),('Indra',D),('Amarāvatī',L),('Gaṅgā',D),('Twenty-one arrows wounding Reṇukā',W)],story=S7,outcome='Rāma actually returns and tends his living, wounded mother before her final instructions and death.')
rel('Reṇukā sons mentioned collectively in80','sons_of',RE,e,'SOURCE_COLLECTIVE_SONS')
power(RE,e,v,mechanism='Rāma’s attributed lament describes her capacity to burn the three worlds.',notes='Attributed praise80.11, not an enacted cosmic destruction or invulnerability.')
e,v=ev(80,'15–22','Reṇukā wipes her son’s tears, says she will remain near him and tells the earlier events: Kṛtavīrya’s son arrived at noon with troops, was honoured and fed through Kāmadhenu, then demanded the cow. When the sage was silent he angrily released her; at contact she generated a powerful four-part army. Royal troops fled and the king repeatedly loosed five hundred arrows but was defeated. She explicitly says he WENT HOME, the cow went to heaven, and he RETURNED to kill the father with one chest-arrow and wound her without offence with twenty-one. This direct mother’s recollection differs from79.35–43’s continuous visit sequence; both are retained.',[(RE,F),(RA,F),(JA,S),(SON,F),(KV,F),(KD,C),('Kārtavīrya four-part hunting army','ARMY'),('Twenty-one arrows wounding Reṇukā',W),('Kārtavīrya heart-piercing arrow in79',W)],story=S7,outcome='Her alternate chronology is recorded as a recollection, without silently inserting a return into79.')
e,v=ev(80,'23–27','Reṇukā commands prompt destruction of the wicked king and making earth without kṣatriyas TWENTY-ONE times because twenty-one arrows were put in her body. She gives another instruction: perform the parents’ funeral rites where no one has ever been cremated, summon all-Veda-knowing Dattātreya, and perform subsequent rites through thirteen days so both attain their destination; no other teacher is equivalent. After speaking she actually abandons her body and reaches a difficult-to-attain abode. The narrator adds that Rāma did all she commanded. That concise fulfillment summary is retained without inventing the unexamined detailed funeral or repeated revenge episodes.',[(RE,F),(RA,F),(JA,S),(SON,F),(DT,S),('Kṣatriyas targeted by Reṇukā command','FAMILY')],story=S7,outcome='Mother’s commands and actual death close the parent-loss episode; the narrator’s broad summary is distinct from later detailed enacted events.')
val(RE,'commanded_revenge','Destroy the king promptly and remove kṣatriyas twenty-one times because she received twenty-one arrows80.23–24; no detailed repeated acts occur in this body.',e)
val(RE,'commanded_funeral','A never-cremated place; summon Dattātreya; subsequent rites through thirteen days for both parents’ destination80.25–26.',e)
power(RE,e,v,mechanism='Actually abandons her body and attains a difficult-to-reach abode after the instructions.',notes='No named final realm is substituted for dhāma durgamam80.27. The funeral/vengeance fulfillment is a concise narrator summary, with later details outside this scope.')
u=note_evidence(80,'21; comparison79.35–43','Reṇukā’s recollection explicitly says the king went home after battle, then returned and killed/wounded her parents80.21–22.79.35–43 narrates the king proceeding to the sage after the cow left, killing him, wounding Reṇukā, and only then returning home. No single reconstructed timeline is imposed.','NEEDS_REVIEW')
